WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.311
Idea. A delivery business buys a van. It will use that van for years. Every week it also pays for fuel and the driver's wages, and those are used up straight away. These are two very different kinds of spending.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:26.697
Capital expenditure is money spent buying or improving non-current assets that the business will use for more than one year. It is shown in the statement of financial position.

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:37.723
Revenue expenditure is money spent on the everyday running of the business. Its benefit is used up within the current year, so it is an expense in the income statement.

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:49.087
Method. To define either term, first say what the money is spent on. Then develop it: how long the benefit lasts, or which financial statement shows it.

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:56.294
For a difference question, write both halves in one sentence, joined by whereas. Each half earns a mark.

00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:05.862
If an example is asked, give a specific item for each. A van or a machine for capital. Wages, fuel or repairs for revenue.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:15.723
Worked example. Here is a real question from November 2025. Explain the term revenue expenditure. Two marks.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:25.535
First the point. Revenue expenditure is money spent on the everyday running costs of the business, such as wages, fuel and repairs.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:39.561
Then develop it. Its benefit is used up within the current year, so it is recorded as an expense in that year's income statement. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:50.981
Common mistake. A common mistake is defining capital expenditure as money the owner puts into the business. That is capital, which is something different.

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:57.799
Another is saying revenue expenditure is money earned. Expenditure always means money spent.

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:06.659
Your turn. Now you try. Kemi owns a bakery. Answer both parts about her spending. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:20.325
Answer. Part a. Capital expenditure is money spent buying or improving non-current assets. The benefit lasts more than one year, so it is shown in the statement of financial position.

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:29.021
Part b. A new oven is capital expenditure. The bakers' wages, or repairs to the oven, are revenue expenditure.

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:37.598
Remember: say what the money is spent on, then how long the benefit lasts, or where it is shown. Now try the practice questions.
